Mobile Credential Readers: Unique digital keys issued to employees or residents for property access. They are on the user's mobile phones. This reduces the cost for 
  building supervisors, as physical keys/ fobs don't have to be produced. Issuing, adjusting or removing privileges can easily be done remotely. This is much easier for
  uncooperative tenants than trying to get a key returned, and unlike a physical key, it cannot be cloned. For apps, when the user wants to enter the door, they must 
  open the app and hold the device up to the reader. The reader uses Wifi, Bluetooth or Near Field Communication to search for a verified credential that matches one stored.
  Source: https://www.avigilon.com/blog/mobile-credentials
  Image Source: https://www.avigilon.com/blog/mobile-credentials
Intercom Systems: Intercoms are communication systems used in buildings, or groups of buildings. They can include video, and are often connected to speakers. These, like
  the ones fitted to some apartment buildings, prevent unwanted access. Anyone wanting to enter the building that doesn't have a key must ring the intercom for the house/
  apartment they wish to enter. If the owner knows/ expects them, they can let them in, but if they don't, the person has no means of accessing the building, keeping 
  out potential thieves.
  Image Source: https://ultrasecuredirect.com/19397-large_default/8-apartment-4g-gsm-intercom.jpg
Turnstiles: Turnstiles are a type of gate that let one person travel through at a time. They are common at train stations, amusement parks and stadiums to ensure that only
  those who have paid are allowed in. Commonly, the only way to get the turnstile to turn is to pay at the gate itself, or by scanning a card such as a SmartRider. They
  range from waist height, to full height. It is relatively easy for people, called fare evaders, to circumvent some models of turnstiles, leading to the full height ones
  used in some subway stations.
  Source: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Turnstile
  Image Source: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Turnstiles_in_Alewife_station%2C_August_2005.jpg/500px-Turnstiles_in_Alewife_station%2C_August_2005.jpg
Bollards: Bollards are a common way of preventing vehicles from accessing areas they are not supposed to. These include concrete and metal versions, commonly installed
  in car parks or on roads to create pedestrian safe environments. Some bollards are fitted underground, retracting into and out of the ground to allow access at certain
  times, and blocking the area in others.
  Image Source: https://wafenceworks.com.au/wp-content/uploads/2021/12/AdobeStock_128123405-Large.jpeg
Interlocking/Airlock Doors: These are systems where two or more doors are electronically connected in such a way that one cannot be opened without the other being shut.
  This creates a small room between the doors that, if the person trying to enter is not allowed to, they cannot pass the second door. This use is seen sometimes in 
  jewellery stores, where the first/ outer door is unlocked, but the person, once the first door is shut, must be 'buzzed in' to access the shop. The principle also 
  prevents piggy-backing, and if a malicious user tries to enter with a valid one, the second door won't open. It also provides a security measure against things getting 
  out, and for this reason are used in aviaries and walk-in cages at zoos. 
  Image Source: https://www.spectra-vision.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/09/Dit-2-1.jpg
